From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] The starting window is a decision to make on the device

Patch 0008 turned the starting window off, for two reasons. The second one --
"the previous screen stays until that frame" -- was not true: the application
transition gave up five seconds in and took the previous screen away while the
incoming window was still DRAW_PENDING, which is what left the two system bars
over nothing for twenty to thirty seconds (patch 0067, and open-items row 16).
The first reason is true and unchanged: a starting window is another window, so
another 6 MB of fresh pages and another full-screen e-paper update.

What that update buys was not known when 0008 was written, and it is the whole
decision. A starting window draws the target activity's own windowBackground,
which the system draws itself, from the package's resources, without waiting
for the application's process to get anywhere. For the application this device
is for, that background is the application's own logo page:

    com.tencent.weread.LauncherActivity
        android:theme = @style/launch_style
            android:windowBackground = @drawable/launch_bg   (a layer-list:
                @drawable/weread_launch, and a bitmap at top=142dp)

(read out of the installed APK with aapt2 on 2026-11-03: `dump xmltree --file
AndroidManifest.xml` for the theme, `dump resources` for the style and the
drawable.)

So the choice is between a reader who taps an application and sees its own logo
within a fraction of a second, and one who sees the screen they tapped from for
the whole of a twenty to thirty second start. The owner has asked for the first
one four times. It is also measured that the application's own logo page never
reaches the screen on its own: its window sits at mDrawState=DRAW_PENDING and
becomes READY_TO_SHOW only in the same two-second sample in which the next
activity's window is added, two seconds before that one has drawn
(RELEASE-PLAN.md, 2026-11-03 03:00). The system can put that page up; the
application cannot.

This patch does not make the choice. It makes it changeable on the device:
debug.pw3.disable_starting_window is read first, and ro.pw3.disable_starting_window
is its default, which is the same pair 0008 already uses for the software canvas.
A release image refuses writes to sys.pw3.* and has no su, but debug.* properties
can be set over adb, so both ways can be looked at on the same device, on the
same screen, in one sitting -- which is how this project settles anything about
what the panel shows. The product's own default moves to false in the same
change (device/amazon/pw3/pw3.mk).

What this does not change: the number of panel updates. Today a cold start is
one update to the bars-over-nothing black and one to the application's content.
With a starting window it is one to the application's logo and one to its
content. The same two; the middle one carries something.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/ActivityRecord.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/ActivityRecord.java
@@ -2166,11 +2166,27 @@
             // We don't show starting window for overlay activities.
             return;
         }
-        if (SystemProperties.getBoolean("ro.pw3.disable_starting_window", false)) {
+        if (SystemProperties.getBoolean("debug.pw3.disable_starting_window",
+                SystemProperties.getBoolean("ro.pw3.disable_starting_window", false))) {
             // PW3: an e-paper update takes 134 ms or more and every window
             // buffer is 6 MB of fresh pages. A preview window would be
             // allocated, drawn and shown only to be replaced by the
             // application's first frame; the previous screen stays instead.
+            //
+            // The debug property is read first, so that the one thing this
+            // decides -- whether the reader sees that an application is
+            // starting -- can be settled on the device, by eye, without a
+            // build. Of the two reasons above the second was not true: until
+            // the transition waited for the first frame (patch 0067) the
+            // previous screen was taken away five seconds in, and what was
+            // left was the two system bars over nothing. The first reason
+            // still holds, and what it buys is the question: a starting
+            // window draws the target activity's own windowBackground, and
+            // for the application this device is for that is its logo
+            // (com.tencent.weread.LauncherActivity has
+            // android:theme=@style/launch_style, whose windowBackground is
+            // the layer-list @drawable/launch_bg), while that application
+            // draws its own first frame twenty to thirty seconds later.
             return;
         }
 
